{
  "_doc": "Wire fixtures for the phase-1 WebSocket messages (Q-91 spec 10.2). Each <type>.<variant>.json file is ONE message exactly as it travels in a text frame. Variants: min = only the fields the Swift encoder always writes; full = every optional field present; other names are the named case. A type with no .full.json has no optional fields. Contract, the same for every peer (Swift: Shared/Tests/WireFixtureTests.swift; Rust: QuipWindows/tests/): decode the file into the peer's own type (Swift: swiftType, from Shared/MessageProtocol.swift and Shared/DisplayGeometry.swift), encode that value again, and the result must have the same keys, the same JSON value kinds AND the same values as the file, recursively, in any key order. Two allowances, no others: (1) a key whose value is null in the file may be absent from the re-encoding (Swift omits nil optionals rather than writing null); (2) numbers compare as numbers, so an integer-valued double may print as 2 or 2.0. Strings compare exactly: UUIDs are upper case, as Swift's JSONEncoder writes them, and a peer must write them the same way.",
